This stylesheet includes the following sections:

* Global styles: These apply to all elements on the page, and set up basic styling for things like box sizing, margins, and padding.
* Header styles: This section sets up the style for the website's header element, including background color, padding, and text alignment.
* Navigation styles: This section sets up the style for the website's navigation menu, including list styling, margin, and padding.
* Content styles: This section sets up the style for the main content area of the page, including max width, margin, padding, and box shadow.
* Article styles: This section sets up the style for individual articles within the content area, including display, width, and margin.
* Footer styles: This section sets up the style for the website's footer element, including background color, padding, and text alignment.

The idea behind cascading styles is that you can define styles at different levels of specificity (e.g. global vs. element-specific), and then let the browser resolve conflicts by applying the most specific rule to each element. In this example, we've defined global styles that apply to all elements on the page, as well as more specific styles for individual elements like the header, navigation, content area, articles, and footer.

