Here is an example of how you can detect duplicates in an array using JavaScript:
```
function detectDuplicates(arr) {
  let seen = {};
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (seen.hasOwnProperty(arr[i])) {
      console.log(`Duplicate found: ${arr[i]}`);
    } else {
      seen[arr[i]] = true;
    }
  }
}

const array = [3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4];
detectDuplicates(array);
```
This code uses an object `seen` to keep track of the elements we've already seen. We iterate through the array and for each element, we check if it's already in the `seen` object. If it is, that means it's a duplicate, so we log a message indicating that.

When you run this code with the input array `[3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4]`, it will output:
```
Duplicate found: 2
Duplicate found: 3
```
This indicates that the elements `2` and `3` appear more than once in the array.

Alternatively, you can use a Set to keep track of unique elements, like this:
```
function detectDuplicates(arr) {
  const seen = new Set();
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (seen.has(arr[i])) {
      console.log(`Duplicate found: ${arr[i]}`);
    } else {
      seen.add(arr[i]);
    }
  }
}

const array = [3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4];
detectDuplicates(array);
```
